@NODE S4 "18.4. Optimizing Dictionary Lookups"
@TITLE "18.4. Optimizing Dictionary Lookups"
@NEXT "S5"
@PREV "S3"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}18.4. Optimizing Dictionary Lookups @{ub}

The second step of the Soundex algorithm is to convert characters to digits in a specific pattern. What's the best way to do this?

The most obvious solution is to define a dictionary with individual characters as keys and their corresponding digits as values, and do dictionary lookups on each character. This is what we have in @{code}soundex/stage1/soundex1c.py@{body} (the current best result so far):

@{code}

charToSoundex = {"A": "9",
"B": "1",
"C": "2",
"D": "3",
"E": "9",
"F": "1",
"G": "2",
"H": "9",
"I": "9",
"J": "2",
"K": "2",
"L": "4",
"M": "5",
"N": "5",
"O": "9",
"P": "1",
"Q": "2",
"R": "6",
"S": "2",
"T": "3",
"U": "9",
"V": "1",
"W": "9",
"X": "2",
"Y": "9",
"Z": "2"}

def soundex(source):
# ... input check omitted for brevity ...
source = source[0].upper() + source[1:]
digits = source[0]
for s in source[1:]:
s = s.upper()
digits += charToSoundex[s]

@{body}

You timed @{code}soundex1c.py@{body} already; this is how it performs:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage1>python soundex1c.py
Woo W000 14.5341678901
Pilgrim P426 19.2650071448
Flingjingwaller F452 30.1003563302

@{body}

This code is straightforward, but is it the best solution? Calling @{code}upper()@{body} on each individual character seems inefficient; it would probably be better to call @{code}upper()@{body} once on the entire string.

Then there's the matter of incrementally building the @{code}digits@{body} string. Incrementally building strings like this is horribly inefficient; internally, the Python interpreter needs to create a new string each time through the loop, then discard the old one.

Python is good at lists, though. It can treat a string as a list of characters automatically. And lists are easy to combine into strings again, using the string method @{code}join()@{body}.

Here is @{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2a.py@{body}, which converts letters to digits by using ? and @{code}lambda@{body}:

@{code}

def soundex(source):
# ...
source = source.upper()
digits = source[0] + "".join(map(lambda c: charToSoundex[c], source[1:]))

@{body}

Surprisingly, @{code}soundex2a.py@{body} is not faster:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage2>python soundex2a.py
Woo W000 15.0097526362
Pilgrim P426 19.254806407
Flingjingwaller F452 29.3790847719

@{body}

The overhead of the anonymous @{code}lambda@{body} function kills any performance you gain by dealing with the string as a list of characters.

@{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2b.py@{body} uses a list comprehension instead of ? and @{code}lambda@{body}:

@{code}

source = source.upper()
digits = source[0] + "".join([charToSoundex[c] for c in source[1:]])

@{body}

Using a list comprehension in @{code}soundex2b.py@{body} is faster than using ? and @{code}lambda@{body} in @{code}soundex2a.py@{body}, but still not faster than the original code (incrementally building a string in @{code}soundex1c.py@{body}):

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage2>python soundex2b.py
Woo W000 13.4221324219
Pilgrim P426 16.4901234654
Flingjingwaller F452 25.8186157738

@{body}

It's time for a radically different approach. Dictionary lookups are a general purpose tool. Dictionary keys can be any length string (or many other data types), but in this case we are only dealing with single-character keys @{i}and@{ui} single-character values. It turns out that Python has a specialized function for handling exactly this situation: the @{code}string.maketrans@{body} function.

This is @{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2c.py@{body}:

@{code}

allChar = string.uppercase + string.lowercase
charToSoundex = string.maketrans(allChar, "91239129922455912623919292" * 2)
def soundex(source):
# ...
digits = source[0].upper() + source[1:].translate(charToSoundex)

@{body}

What the heck is going on here? @{code}string.maketrans@{body} creates a translation matrix between two strings: the first argument and the second argument. In this case, the first argument is the string @{code}ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz@{body}, and the second argument is the string @{code}9123912992245591262391929291239129922455912623919292@{body}. See the pattern? It's the same conversion pattern we were setting up longhand with a dictionary. A maps to 9, B maps to 1, C maps to 2, and so forth. But it's not a dictionary; it's a specialized data structure that you can access using the string method @{code}translate@{body}, which translates each character into the corresponding digit, according to the matrix defined by @{code}string.maketrans@{body}.

@{code}timeit@{body} shows that @{code}soundex2c.py@{body} is significantly faster than defining a dictionary and looping through the input and building the output incrementally:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage2>python soundex2c.py
Woo W000 11.437645008
Pilgrim P426 13.2825062962
Flingjingwaller F452 18.5570110168

@{body}

You're not going to get much better than that. Python has a specialized function that does exactly what you want to do; use it and move on.

@{b}Example 18.4. Best Result So Far: @{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2c.py@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

import string, re

allChar = string.uppercase + string.lowercase
charToSoundex = string.maketrans(allChar, "91239129922455912623919292" * 2)
isOnlyChars = re.compile('^[A-Za-z]+$').search

def soundex(source):
if not isOnlyChars(source):
return "0000"
digits = source[0].upper() + source[1:].translate(charToSoundex)
digits2 = digits[0]
for d in digits[1:]:
if digits2[-1] != d:
digits2 += d
digits3 = re.sub('9', '', digits2)
while len(digits3) < 4:
digits3 += "0"
return digits3[:4]

if __name__ == '__main__':
from timeit import Timer
names = ('Woo', 'Pilgrim', 'Flingjingwaller')
for name in names:
statement = "soundex('%s')" % name
t = Timer(statement, "from __main__ import soundex")
print name.ljust(15), soundex(name), min(t.repeat())

@{body}

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S5 "18.5. Optimizing List Operations"
@TITLE "18.5. Optimizing List Operations"
@NEXT "S6"
@PREV "S4"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}18.5. Optimizing List Operations @{ub}

The third step in the Soundex algorithm is eliminating consecutive duplicate digits. What's the best way to do this?

Here's the code we have so far, in @{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2c.py@{body}:

@{code}

digits2 = digits[0]
for d in digits[1:]:
if digits2[-1] != d:
digits2 += d

@{body}

Here are the performance results for @{code}soundex2c.py@{body}:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage2>python soundex2c.py
Woo W000 12.6070768771
Pilgrim P426 14.4033353401
Flingjingwaller F452 19.7774882003

@{body}

The first thing to consider is whether it's efficient to check @{code}digits[-1]@{body} each time through the loop. Are list indexes expensive? Would we be better off maintaining the last digit in a separate variable, and checking that instead?

To answer this question, here is @{code}soundex/stage3/soundex3a.py@{body}:

@{code}

digits2 = ''
last_digit = ''
for d in digits:
if d != last_digit:
digits2 += d
last_digit = d

@{body}

@{code}soundex3a.py@{body} does not run any faster than @{code}soundex2c.py@{body}, and may even be slightly slower (although it's not enough of a difference to say for sure):

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage3>python soundex3a.py
Woo W000 11.5346048171
Pilgrim P426 13.3950636184
Flingjingwaller F452 18.6108927252

@{body}

Why isn't @{code}soundex3a.py@{body} faster? It turns out that list indexes in Python are extremely efficient. Repeatedly accessing @{code}digits2[-1]@{body} is no problem at all. On the other hand, manually maintaining the last seen digit in a separate variable means we have @{i}two@{ui} variable assignments for each digit we're storing, which wipes out any small gains we might have gotten from eliminating the list lookup.

Let's try something radically different. If it's possible to treat a string as a list of characters, it should be possible to use a list comprehension to iterate through the list. The problem is, the code needs access to the previous character in the list, and that's not easy to do with a straightforward list comprehension.

However, it is possible to create a list of index numbers using the built-in @{code}range()@{body} function, and use those index numbers to progressively search through the list and pull out each character that is different from the previous character. That will give you a list of characters, and you can use the string method @{code}join()@{body} to reconstruct a string from that.

Here is @{code}soundex/stage3/soundex3b.py@{body}:

@{code}

digits2 = "".join([digits[i] for i in range(len(digits))
if i == 0 or digits[i-1] != digits[i]])

@{body}

Is this faster? In a word, no.

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage3>python soundex3b.py
Woo W000 14.2245271396
Pilgrim P426 17.8337165757
Flingjingwaller F452 25.9954005327

@{body}

It's possible that the techniques so far as have been "string-centric". Python can convert a string into a list of characters with a single command: @{code}list('abc')@{body} returns @{code}['a', 'b', 'c']@{body}. Furthermore, lists can be @{i}modified in place@{ui} very quickly. Instead of incrementally building a new list (or string) out of the source string, why not move elements around within a single list?

Here is @{code}soundex/stage3/soundex3c.py@{body}, which modifies a list in place to remove consecutive duplicate elements:

@{code}

digits = list(source[0].upper() + source[1:].translate(charToSoundex))
i=0
for item in digits:
if item==digits[i]: continue
i+=1
digits[i]=item
del digits[i+1:]
digits2 = "".join(digits)

@{body}

Is this faster than @{code}soundex3a.py@{body} or @{code}soundex3b.py@{body}? No, in fact it's the slowest method yet:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage3>python soundex3c.py
Woo W000 14.1662554878
Pilgrim P426 16.0397885765
Flingjingwaller F452 22.1789341942

@{body}

We haven't made any progress here at all, except to try and rule out several "clever" techniques. The fastest code we've seen so far was the original, most straightforward method (@{code}soundex2c.py@{body}). Sometimes it doesn't pay to be clever.

@{b}Example 18.5. Best Result So Far: @{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2c.py@{body}@{ub}

@{code}

import string, re

allChar = string.uppercase + string.lowercase
charToSoundex = string.maketrans(allChar, "91239129922455912623919292" * 2)
isOnlyChars = re.compile('^[A-Za-z]+$').search

def soundex(source):
if not isOnlyChars(source):
return "0000"
digits = source[0].upper() + source[1:].translate(charToSoundex)
digits2 = digits[0]
for d in digits[1:]:
if digits2[-1] != d:
digits2 += d
digits3 = re.sub('9', '', digits2)
while len(digits3) < 4:
digits3 += "0"
return digits3[:4]

if __name__ == '__main__':
from timeit import Timer
names = ('Woo', 'Pilgrim', 'Flingjingwaller')
for name in names:
statement = "soundex('%s')" % name
t = Timer(statement, "from __main__ import soundex")
print name.ljust(15), soundex(name), min(t.repeat())

@{body}

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S6 "18.6. Optimizing String Manipulation"
@TITLE "18.6. Optimizing String Manipulation"
@NEXT "S7"
@PREV "S5"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}18.6. Optimizing String Manipulation @{ub}

The final step of the Soundex algorithm is padding short results with zeros, and truncating long results. What is the best way to do this?

This is what we have so far, taken from @{code}soundex/stage2/soundex2c.py@{body}:

@{code}

digits3 = re.sub('9', '', digits2)
while len(digits3) python soundex2c.py
Woo W000 12.6070768771
Pilgrim P426 14.4033353401
Flingjingwaller F452 19.7774882003

@{body}

The first thing to consider is replacing that regular expression with a loop. This code is from @{code}soundex/stage4/soundex4a.py@{body}:

@{code}

digits3 = ''
for d in digits2:
if d != '9':
digits3 += d

@{body}

Is @{code}soundex4a.py@{body} faster? Yes it is:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage4>python soundex4a.py
Woo W000 6.62865531792
Pilgrim P426 9.02247576158
Flingjingwaller F452 13.6328416042

@{body}

But wait a minute. A loop to remove characters from a string? We can use a simple string method for that. Here's @{code}soundex/stage4/soundex4b.py@{body}:

@{code}

digits3 = digits2.replace('9', '')

@{body}

Is @{code}soundex4b.py@{body} faster? That's an interesting question. It depends on the input:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage4>python soundex4b.py
Woo W000 6.75477414029
Pilgrim P426 7.56652144337
Flingjingwaller F452 10.8727729362

@{body}

The string method in @{code}soundex4b.py@{body} is faster than the loop for most names, but it's actually slightly slower than @{code}soundex4a.py@{body} in the trivial case (of a very short name). Performance optimizations aren't always uniform; tuning that makes one case faster can sometimes make other cases slower. In this case, the majority of cases will benefit from the change, so let's leave it at that, but the principle is an important one to remember.

Last but not least, let's examine the final two steps of the algorithm: padding short results with zeros, and truncating long results to four characters. The code you see in @{code}soundex4b.py@{body} does just that, but it's horribly inefficient. Take a look at @{code}soundex/stage4/soundex4c.py@{body} to see why:

@{code}

digits3 += '000'
return digits3[:4]

@{body}

Why do we need a @{code}while@{body} loop to pad out the result? We know in advance that we're going to truncate the result to four characters, and we know that we already have at least one character (the initial letter, which is passed unchanged from the original @{code}source@{body} variable). That means we can simply add three zeros to the output, then truncate it. Don't get stuck in a rut over the exact wording of the problem; looking at the problem slightly differently can lead to a simpler solution.

How much speed do we gain in @{code}soundex4c.py@{body} by dropping the @{code}while@{body} loop? It's significant:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage4>python soundex4c.py
Woo W000 4.89129791636
Pilgrim P426 7.30642134685
Flingjingwaller F452 10.689832367

@{body}

Finally, there is still one more thing you can do to these three lines of code to make them faster: you can combine them into one line. Take a look at @{code}soundex/stage4/soundex4d.py@{body}:

@{code}

return (digits2.replace('9', '') + '000')[:4]

@{body}

Putting all this code on one line in @{code}soundex4d.py@{body} is barely faster than @{code}soundex4c.py@{body}:

@{code}

C:\\samples\\soundex\\stage4>python soundex4d.py
Woo W000 4.93624105857
Pilgrim P426 7.19747593619
Flingjingwaller F452 10.5490700634

@{body}

It is also significantly less readable, and for not much performance gain. Is that worth it? I hope you have good comments. Performance isn't everything. Your optimization efforts must always be balanced against threats to your program's readability and maintainability.

@{" Chapter contents " LINK "MAIN"}

@ENDNODE

@NODE S7 "18.7. Summary"
@TITLE "18.7. Summary"
@NEXT "MAIN"
@PREV "S6"
@TOC "MAIN"
@SMARTWRAP

@{b}18.7. Summary @{ub}

This chapter has illustrated several important aspects of performance tuning in Python, and performance tuning in general.

* If you need to choose between regular expressions and writing a loop, choose regular expressions. The regular expression engine is compiled in C and runs natively on your computer; your loop is written in Python and runs through the Python interpreter.

* If you need to choose between regular expressions and string methods, choose string methods. Both are compiled in C, so choose the simpler one.

* General-purpose dictionary lookups are fast, but specialtiy functions such as @{code}string.maketrans@{body} and string methods such as @{code}isalpha()@{body} are faster. If Python has a custom-tailored function for you, use it.

* Don't be too clever. Sometimes the most obvious algorithm is also the fastest.

* Don't sweat it too much. Performance isn't everything.

I can't emphasize that last point strongly enough. Over the course of this chapter, you made this function three times faster and saved 20 seconds over 1 million function calls. Great. Now think: over the course of those million function calls, how many seconds will your surrounding application wait for a database connection? Or wait for disk I/O? Or wait for user input? Don't spend too much time over-optimizing one algorithm, or you'll ignore obvious improvements somewhere else. Develop an instinct for the sort of code that Python runs well, correct obvious blunders if you find them, and leave the rest alone.

@{b}<a

